<section class="phero"><div class="phero-bg"><img src="/assets/img/photos/tornado-damage.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/tornado-damage-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/tornado-damage.webp 1024w" sizes="100vw" width="1024" height="768" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.18"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><ol><li><a href="/">Home</a></li><li><a href="/property-claim-services/">Services</a></li><li aria-current="page">Windstorm &amp; Tornado Appraisal</li></ol></nav><p class="kicker">Appraisal service</p><h1>Maryland Wind Damage &amp; Tornado Claim Appraisal</h1><p class="lede">Maryland wind damage appraisal for losses from derechos, severe thunderstorms and tornadoes. We document what the wind actually did – including the damage that only shows up once you look past the obvious.</p><div class="btns"><a class="btn brass" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn ghost-l" href="/start-a-claim-review/">Request a free review</a></div><dl class="ph-facts"><div><dt>Perils</dt><dd>Derecho, gusts, tornado</dd></div><div><dt>Frequent gap</dt><dd>Partial siding and roof scope</dd></div><div><dt>Check first</dt><dd>Which deductible applies</dd></div></dl></div></section><div class="wrap two-col"><article class="prose"><h2>Maryland wind events that shape claims</h2>
<p>Maryland doesn’t get many headline tornadoes, but wind losses are routine. Three events still come up when people talk about wind claims here:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>The La Plata tornado, April 28, 2002.</strong> An F4 that cut through Charles County and the town of La Plata, a reminder that Southern Maryland can see violent tornadoes.</li>
<li><strong>The June 29, 2012 derecho.</strong> Straight-line winds swept across the state, toppling trees, damaging roofs and leaving many households without power for days.</li>
<li><strong>The September 1, 2021 tornado.</strong> Spawned by the remnants of Ida, it moved through Annapolis and Edgewater, damaging homes and businesses along its path.</li>
</ul>
<p>Most Maryland tornadoes are brief and fall in the EF0 to EF2 range, and most wind claims come from ordinary thunderstorm gusts. That makes wind damage easy to underestimate: the house is still standing, so the estimate covers a few shingles and a section of gutter.</p>
<h2>What gets missed after a wind event</h2>
<p>Wind works differently from hail. It pulls, twists and pressurizes, and the damage it leaves can be subtle. Common omissions in carrier estimates include:</p>
<ul>
<li>creased or unsealed shingles that look flat but have lost their bond;</li>
<li>siding that has been pulled loose at the nailing strip on one elevation, with no matching product for a partial repair;</li>
<li>racked fences, bent porch posts and lifted decking;</li>
<li>sheathing and rafter damage under a roof that was “only” missing a few tabs;</li>
<li>water intrusion through openings created during the storm;</li>
<li>debris removal and temporary protection such as tarping and board-up.</li>
</ul>
<p>In tornado and derecho files, falling trees are often part of the loss too. Our <a href="/property-claim-services/tree-impact-claim-appraisal/">tree impact claim appraisal</a> page covers removal limits and structural repairs.</p>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Which deductible applies?</b>Some Maryland policies carry a separate windstorm, named-storm or hurricane deductible, often a percentage of the dwelling limit, alongside the regular all-perils deductible. A tornado spun off by a tropical system may or may not trigger the special deductible depending on how the policy defines it. Read the definition before assuming.</div>
<h2>Building a wind estimate that holds up</h2>
<p>For a panel to accept a wider scope, the estimate has to show why the damage is storm-related and why the repair has to be as large as it is. On wind files we focus on:</p>
<ol>
<li>Directional damage patterns that line up with the storm’s track and the reported wind direction.</li>
<li>Close-up photos of creases, torn mat and failed seals on each slope.</li>
<li>Siding and trim product identification, so matching can be addressed with evidence.</li>
<li>Framing checks in the attic or behind removed finishes where the structure may have moved.</li>
<li>Emergency services invoices tied to dates and actual work.</li>
</ol>
<p>Maryland Insurance Appraiser works wind and tornado files throughout the state, from <a href="/service-locations/annapolis/">Annapolis</a> waterfront neighborhoods to subdivisions around <a href="/service-locations/waldorf/">Waldorf</a> in Charles County.</p>
<h2>Timing after a storm</h2>
<p>After widespread wind events, contractors and adjusters are stretched thin, and early estimates are often written quickly. If you received a payment soon after a storm and later found more damage, that additional scope can usually be raised with the carrier as a supplement. If the carrier still disagrees on the amount, appraisal is one way to resolve it. Check your policy’s time limits and keep records of every contact.</p>
<p>It also helps to keep any local storm reports, power-outage notices or neighborhood photos from the day of the event. They place the wind at your address on that date, which matters when a carrier suggests the damage came from age or an earlier storm.</p><h2>Questions about windstorm &amp; tornado appraisal</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Was the 2012 derecho treated as a hurricane under Maryland policies?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="a"><p>Generally no, because a derecho is not a named tropical storm. Policies define the triggers for hurricane or named-storm deductibles differently, though, so the specific wording on your declarations page controls.</p></div></details><details><summary>Can tornado damage to my roof and siding be appraised together?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="a"><p>Yes. When the insurer accepts the claim and disputes the amount, the dwelling repairs, other structures and related items can all be submitted to appraisal as part of the same loss.</p></div></details><details><summary>My adjuster says the siding is only damaged on one side. What now?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="a"><p>If replacement siding for that elevation can’t reasonably match the rest of the house, how much has to be replaced becomes a valuation question. An appraiser documents the product and the mismatch so the panel can decide.</p></div></details></div><h2>Related services</h2><div class="rel"><a href="/property-claim-services/hurricane-noreaster-appraisal/"><small>Service</small><span>Hurricane &amp; Nor’easter Appraisal</span><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a><a href="/property-claim-services/water-damage-appraisal/"><small>Service</small><span>Water Damage Appraisal</span><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a><a href="/property-claim-services/fire-smoke-damage-appraisal/"><small>Service</small><span>Fire &amp; Smoke Damage Appraisal</span><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a></div></article><aside class="side"><div class="side-in"><div class="box box-call"><h2 class="box-h">Talk to an appraiser</h2><p>A free first conversation about your estimate — and a straight answer on whether appraisal fits.</p><a class="box-tel" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg>(844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn brass block" href="/start-a-claim-review/">Send claim details</a><small>Mon–Fri, 8am–6pm ET</small></div><div class="box"><h2 class="box-h">All services</h2><ul class="box-list"><li><a href="/property-claim-services/appraisal-demand-representation/">Appraisal Demand Representation<svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M9 18l6-6-6-6"/></svg></a></li><li><a href="/property-claim-services/home-insurance-appraisal/">Home Insurance Appraisal<svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><